From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kristof Imeri <kristof@lanth.me>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 07:16:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 16/52] wayland: tell sandboxed clients apart with
 security-context-v1

Implement wp_security_context_manager_v1 so sandbox engines such as
Flatpak can open a listening socket for their apps and attach the
engine, app ID and instance ID to every connection made through it.

Every client is sorted when it connects. Connections through a security
context are sandboxed, as the app the context names. Any other
connection is judged by the process that connected, pinned by a pidfd
taken from the socket: a process whose root holds a .flatpak-info is
sandboxed as the app named there, which is how Flatpak and Luft's own
sandbox mark every process they start, and a process without one runs
outside a sandbox. A process that keeps others out of its memory hides
its root, so it counts as outside a sandbox when its mount table, which
anyone may read, has nothing mounted at /.flatpak-info. Clients that cannot be judged, because the process
already exited or its sandbox info cannot be read, count as sandboxed.

The shell tells the compositor which sandboxed apps hold which
permissions with meta_context_set_permitted_apps(), and
meta_context_is_app_permitted() answers for one app. Apps given
"full-access" count as unsandboxed. Connections keep how they were
sorted when the list changes.

Only unsandboxed clients see the manager, so sandboxed apps can neither
nest security contexts nor claim another app's ID. Protocols that should
stay out of sandboxes filter their globals with
meta_wayland_filter_unsandboxed().
